When a new user opens the authentication page without an existing authenticated session, display the Registration Page by default.

The registration page must contain:

### A. Google OAuth Registration

Display a Continue with Google button.

Google OAuth should support both new user registration and existing user authentication.

Expected behavior:

- If the Google account is being used for the first time, create a new Citizen account.
- If the Google account is already registered, authenticate the existing Citizen account.
- Do not create duplicate accounts for the same verified Google email.
- Never allow Google OAuth registration to create Ward Councillor, Department Staff, or System Administrator accounts.

### B. Manual Registration Form

Provide a registration form containing the necessary fields:

- Full Name
- Email Address
- Username
- Password
- Confirm Password

Implement appropriate validation:

- All required fields must be validated.
- Email must have a valid format.
- Email must be unique.
- Username must be unique.
- Password must satisfy appropriate security requirements.
- Confirm Password must match Password.
- Display meaningful validation and error messages.

Only collect information required to create the initial account.

Do not unnecessarily request ward details or residential information during account registration.

### C. Navigation to Login

At the bottom of the registration form, display this message:

"Already have an account? Log in"

When the user clicks the Log in link, navigate to the Citizen Login Page.

## 2.2 Citizen Login Page

This page is exclusively for users who have already registered.

It should contain:

### A. Google OAuth Login

Provide the same Continue with Google button.

If the Google account is already registered, authenticate the existing account.

If the account does not exist, do not silently create an account from the Login Page. Direct the user to the Registration Page.

### B. Manual Login Form

Required fields:

- Email Address or Username
- Password

Include:

- Login button
- Forgot Password link (if supported by the existing authentication architecture)
- Registration navigation link

At the bottom, display:

"Don't have an account? Register"

Clicking Register should navigate to the Registration Page.

## 2.3 Google OAuth: Shared Authentication Logic

Google OAuth must work on both Registration and Login Pages.

Use a common OAuth authentication implementation instead of creating two separate Google authentication systems.

The backend should identify whether the Google account belongs to:

- A new user
- An existing Citizen
- An invited Ward Councillor
- An invited Department Staff member
- An existing System Administrator

Apply role-specific authorization rules after successful identity verification.

An existing invited employee must be able to authenticate through the Google account associated with their invitation.

Do not allow users to change their role through the frontend.

## 2.4 Role Restrictions

There are four roles in the application:

1. Citizen
2. Ward Councillor
3. Department Staff
4. System Administrator

The registration process must be restricted as follows:

**Citizen:**
- Citizens can register independently.
- Citizens can use manual registration or Google OAuth.
- Every newly registered public account must receive the Citizen role.
- Citizens must never be able to select or assign themselves another role.

**Ward Councillor:**
- Cannot register through the public registration form.
- Must receive an invitation from the System Administrator.

**Department Staff:**
- Cannot register through the public registration form.
- Must receive an invitation from the System Administrator.

**System Administrator:**
- Cannot register through the public registration form.
- Existing authorized System Administrator account creation and management mechanisms must remain protected.

Enforce these restrictions in the backend, not just through frontend UI restrictions.

